  "knowledgePattern": "Reflect the interval and pair complementary heights",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Reflect across the interval’s midpoint",
      "body": "<p>Sketch <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and its reflection in the middle of the integration interval.</p>",
      "recap": "This reflects the graph about the middle of the interval.",
      "pitfall": "Put the whole new input inside tangent before simplifying.",
      "pause": "Write the reflected height, then simplify it using the given identity.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l08",
          "reminder": "A horizontal reflection preserves the area of the matching region."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Add the two heights",
      "body": "<p>Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a common denominator.</p>",
      "recap": "The original graph and its reflection have equal integrals on this interval.",
      "pitfall": "You are adding two copies of the unknown integral.",
      "pause": "Simplify the sum, then use its area to find the original integral.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l21",
          "reminder": "Adding integrands adds their signed integrals over the same limits."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> on the interior. Reflecting in the midpoint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> preserves the area over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so f and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> have the same integral.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The supplied identity gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence the reflected heights add to 1 throughout the interior.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Adding the two equal integrals gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, answer B.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The integrand can also be written <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> on the interval, extending continuously to value 0 at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. This handles the endpoint where tangent itself is undefined. No trigonometric antiderivative is needed.</p>"
}
